1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,450
When practicing a skill,

2
00:00:01,450 --> 00:00:05,100
doing the exact same drill a hundred times feels productive.

3
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:10,370
But your brain actually learns best when you never repeat the exact same movement.

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:15,650
In the nineteen thirties, neurophysiologist Nikolai Bernstein studied master blacksmiths.

5
00:00:15,650 --> 00:00:19,050
The hammer hit the exact same millimeter every time,

6
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,350
yet the path of their arms and joints was different on every single strike.

7
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:27,140
He called this repetition without repetition.

8
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:30,250
Your brain does not wire rigid, robotic tracks.

9
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:33,200
Neuroplasticity works by solving problems.

10
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:39,630
When you mindlessly repeat an identical motion, your nervous system goes on autopilot and stops adapting.

11
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:44,420
True mastery is training your brain to find the solution through changing conditions.

12
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:46,400
So stop doing identical reps.

13
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,700
Keep your target constant, but vary the execution:

14
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:53,500
shift your stance, change the tempo, adjust your angle.

15
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:56,000
Repeat the goal, never the movement.

16
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,440
That is how you build real skill.
